<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.13.4: Solving Inequality Problems Using Tables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00611</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.13.4: Solving Inequality Problems Using Tables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c58a752d-7e07-47dd-bac5-7a99239ca145</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>For questions 1 – 5, use the following scenario to graph an inequality from a table:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>Andre is making trail mix with dried fruit and mixed nuts. Dried fruit costs $3 per pound and mixed nuts cost $6 per pound. He wants to spend no more than $54 on the trail mix. The inequality \(y \leq −2x + 18\) represents the number of pounds of dried fruit, \(y\), he can buy for \(x\) pounds of nuts.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>The related equation \(y = -2x + 18\) represents the boundary line of the inequality. Complete the table of values to find points on the boundary line of the inequality.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>18</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>14</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the data from your table of values that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Is the boundary line for the inequality \(y \leq −2x + 18\) a solid line or a dashed line? Explain how you know.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The line is solid because the inequality \(\leq\) means the points on the line are included as solutions.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Is the point \((0, 0)\) a solution to the inequality?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Yes, \(0 \leq −2(0) + 18\), \(0 \leq 18\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Use the graph from number 2. Enter the inequality \(y \leq −2x + 18\) to represent the data. Notice the shading for the solution region of this inequality.</para>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>In problems 6–10, use the data from the table to graph the inequality and write an inequality with two variables.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>-2</entry>
            <entry>-5</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>0</entry>
            <entry>-6</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>2</entry>
            <entry>1</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>3</entry>
            <entry>5</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the data from your table of values that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--Q#6-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_04.png"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para> Now, in the Desmos window graph \(y=x\).</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_05.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>How does the line need to move to capture all of the points?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Answers may vary.</para>
          <para>Increase the y-intercept or the slope to capture the point \((4, 5)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Using desmos, adjust the slope and/or the \(y\)-intercept to find an inequality that could be a possibility.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Answers may vary.</para>
          <para>These are possible representation of where the line could on the graph</para>
          <div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
            <para><emphasis>Less than</emphasis>
        
        \(y&lt;2x\)
        <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_06.png"/></media></figure></para>
            <para><emphasis>Less than or equal to</emphasis>
        
        \(y^2x-1\)
        <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_07.png"/></media></figure></para>
            <para><emphasis>Greater than</emphasis>
        
        \(y&gt;x-7\)
        <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_08.png"/></media></figure></para>
            <para><emphasis>Greater than or equal to</emphasis>
        \(y&gt;x-6\)
        <figure><media alt="ea43ef5b5a2f10d594e518d4673515e5c32687a6 (234×306)"><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_09.png"/></media></figure><emphasis> </emphasis></para>
            <para>
              <emphasis>Not a solution</emphasis>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_10.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </div>
          <newline/>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Use the graph from number 7. Shade the correct side of the boundary line to represent the inequality \(y &gt; 3x − 2\).</para>
            <para> When you have finished plotting the points, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_11.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
